    "notes": "I implemented the spec in `src/solution.py` without changing its names or signatures. All tests now pass: 71 of 71 public and 159 of 159 private.\n\n- **`parse_duration`** strips leading and trailing whitespace, then reads components one at a time. Each component must be a count made of ASCII digits with no leading zeros (`0` alone is allowed), followed by a lowercase unit; `ms` is checked before `m` and `s`. Units must run from `d` down to `ms`, each used at most once. The total is checked against 2^53 − 1 as it builds up, and a total over that raises an error instead of being clamped. Any invalid input raises `ValueError` with \"duration\" in the message.\n- **`format_duration`** accepts only a true `int` from 0 to 2^53 − 1, so it rejects bools and floats. It works down from days to milliseconds, leaves out zero components, and returns `\"0ms\"` for zero. Invalid input raises `ValueError` with \"milliseconds\" in the message.\n\nMy first version failed one private test: a count with thousands of digits hit Python's built-in limit on converting long digit strings to `int`, which raised an error without \"duration\" in the message. Now any count with more digits than 2^53 − 1 is rejected as too large before conversion. This is the spec's own rule, so the fix didn't involve bending the code to fit the test.\n\nTests left failing: none",
